Below is language-agnostic pseudocode for the contiguous dynamic-programming (DP) solver.
It returns the minimum-size partition of the sorted input such that each block (a contiguous slice) obeys your three rules and contains ≤ k numbers.

⸻

Helper — legality test for one slice

FUNCTION LEGAL(A, l, r, params):
    # A is the sorted array
    # slice is A[l] … A[r-1]   (size = r-l, guaranteed 1 ≤ size ≤ k)
    x1  ← A[l]                 # first element of the slice
    G   ← 0                    # running gcd

    # --- rule 3: all large or all small ---------------------------
    if  (min(A[l..r-1])   ≥ params.lots_to_group)  OR
        (max(A[l..r-1])   < params.lots_to_group):
        pass            # ok
    else:
        RETURN False

    # --- check every xj in the slice ------------------------------
    FOR j from l TO r-1:
        xj   ← A[j]

        # rule 1
        ratio ← xj / x1
        IF ratio > params.max_ratio            THEN RETURN False
        IF ratio does NOT have exactly params.decimals
           decimal places                      THEN RETURN False

        # rule 2  (update gcd incrementally)
        G ← gcd(G, xj)
        q ← xj / G
        IF q < params.interval_low OR q > params.interval_high
           THEN RETURN False

    RETURN True

(Change the decimal-digit test if you want “at most” instead of “exactly.”)

⸻

Step 1 – sort and pre-compute legality table

INPUT: array NUMS[0…N−1], integer k, parameter bundle P

A ← sort(NUMS ascending)

CREATE a Boolean table OK[0…N-1][0…N]  # default False
FOR l from 0 TO N-1:
    FOR r from l+1 TO min(l+k, N):     # slice size ≤ k
        OK[l][r] ← LEGAL(A, l, r, P)

Cost: O(N · k · slice_check).
If you cache the running gcd and reuse earlier work, slice_check is O(slice size), so overall ≈ O(N · k²).

⸻

Step 2 – DP for “minimum number of groups”

INF ← very large
dp[0…N]   ←  INF      # dp[i] = min groups needed to cover A[i…N-1]
next[0…N] ← −1
dp[N]     ← 0         # empty suffix needs 0 groups

FOR i from N−1 DOWNTO 0:
    FOR r from i+1 TO min(i+k, N):
        IF OK[i][r]  AND  dp[r] + 1 < dp[i]:
            dp[i]   ← dp[r] + 1
            next[i] ← r

Complexity: O(N · k).

⸻

Step 3 – reconstruct the optimal partition

i ← 0
PARTITION ← empty list

WHILE i < N:
    j ← next[i]           # end of the chosen group (exclusive)
    IF j = −1:            # should not happen, fallback to singleton
        j ← i + 1
    APPEND A[i…j-1] to PARTITION
    i ← j

RETURN PARTITION


⸻

Big-picture costs & limits

Piece	Time	Space
Precompute OK	O(N · k²) (worst case)	O(N · k) booleans
DP + backtrack	O(N · k)	O(N) integers

With N in the low thousands and k ≲ 10, this runs in milliseconds in any mainstream language. If k is large, prune the inner loops early (the first illegal extension breaks that row of OK).

⸻

Usage reminder
	•	Feed the function the sorted list; the algorithm forces every group to be a contiguous slice of that order.
	•	Single-element groups are always legal, so the DP never gets stuck.
	•	Modify LEGAL if you later relax/tweak the decimal or ratio rules—the DP itself stays unchanged.